
  Buffer in memory; flush when any of: 20 records queued, 5 seconds elapsed, an [38;5;153merror[39m-level record arrives (flush
  immediately — the crash is the thing you need), or the page/process is going away.

  Transport is [38;5;153mnavigator.sendBeacon[39m for the unload flush specifically, because [38;5;153mfetch[39m isn't reliably delivered during
  teardown, and [38;5;153mfetch(..., { keepalive: true })[39m otherwise. Payload is [38;5;153m{ sessionId, env, records: [...] }[39m.

  The failure behavior matters more than the happy path, because a logger that degrades the app it's debugging is worse
  than no logger:

  - [1mNever throws into caller code.[0m Every sink call is wrapped; a sink failure can't break a login.
  - [1mNo retry storm.[0m A failed batch is dropped, not requeued. A [38;5;153mdropped[39m counter rides along in the next successful
    envelope so you know the record stream has holes.
  - [1mBounded buffer[0m at 200 records, dropping oldest. A long-lived tab with a dead endpoint can't grow memory without
    limit.
  - [1mCircuit breaker.[0m After 3 consecutive failures, stop trying for 60s. Otherwise a down endpoint means every user's
    browser POSTs at it every 5 seconds indefinitely — you'd be self-inflicting load during exactly the incident you're
    trying to debug.

  Config: the remote sink is only wired if an endpoint URL is configured. Unset means console-only, so local development
  needs no setup and can't accidentally ship records anywhere.

